            <ArticleTitle>Longitudinal evaluation of milk type consumed and weight status in preschoolers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>335-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/archdischild-2012-302941</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate relationships between type of milk consumed and weight status among preschool children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Longitudinal cohort study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The Early Childhood Longitudinal Study, Birth Cohort, a representative sample of US children.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">10 700 US children examined at age 2 and 4 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Body mass index (BMI) z score and overweight/obese status as a function of milk type intake.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The majority of children drank whole or 2% milk (87% at 2 years, 79.3% at 4 years). Across racial/ethnic and socio-economic status subgroups, 1%/skim milk drinkers had higher BMI z scores than 2%/whole milk drinkers. In multivariable analyses, increasing fat content in the type of milk consumed was inversely associated with BMI z score (p&lt;0.0001). Compared to those drinking 2%/whole milk, 2- and 4-year-old children drinking 1%/skim milk had an increased adjusted odds of being overweight (age 2 OR 1.64, p&lt;0.0001; age 4 OR 1.63, p&lt;0.0001) or obese (age 2 OR 1.57, p&lt;0.01; age 4 OR 1.64, p&lt;0.0001). In longitudinal analysis, children drinking 1%/skim milk at both 2 and 4 years were more likely to become overweight/obese between these time points (adjusted OR 1.57, p&lt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Consumption of 1%/skim milk is more common among overweight/obese preschoolers, potentially reflecting the choice of parents to give overweight/obese children low-fat milk to drink. Nevertheless, 1%/skim milk does not appear to restrain body weight gain between 2 and 4 years of age in this age range, emphasising a need for weight-targeted recommendations with a stronger evidence base.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
